<edge source="&quot;ADMINISTRATIVE AGENT&quot;" target="&quot;LENDER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">194.0</data>
  <data key="d5">The roles and responsibilities of the "ADMINISTRATIVE AGENT" and the "LENDER" in relation to the credit agreement are intricately connected, underscoring a collaborative framework designed to ensure effective communication, compliance, and administrative management throughout the loan process.

The "ADMINISTRATIVE AGENT" operates on behalf of the "LENDER" and is primarily responsible for overseeing compliance with the terms of the loan agreement. This includes determining the status of lenders, identifying any defaulting lenders, and communicating this information to both the borrower and other lenders. The agent acts as a facilitator of communication between the borrower and the lender, ensuring that financial matters, requests, and obligations are clearly conveyed and adhered to.

Furthermore, the "ADMINISTRATIVE AGENT" manages various operational aspects of the loan documents, coordinating with the lender to ensure proper management and distribution of funds as dictated by the credit agreement. This includes managing loan disbursements, handling erroneous payment situations, and reallocation of revolving loans and letters of credit obligations among the lenders. In addition, the agent is responsible for notifying the lender regarding compliance responsibilities under regulations such as the U.S. Patriot Act and the Beneficial Ownership Regulation.

The "LENDER," in turn, collaborates with the "ADMINISTRATIVE AGENT" to fulfill its administrative obligations and compliance requirements related to the loan. The lender interacts with the agent to coordinate the terms and operations of the credit agreement, ensuring that the administration, participation, and performance of the loans and related agreements are effectively managed. This cooperation allows the lender to focus on the financial aspects while the administrative agent handles the intricate details necessary for compliance and operational efficiency.

In summary, the relationship between the "ADMINISTRATIVE AGENT" and the "LENDER" is characterized by a shared responsibility for managing the credit agreement, with the administrative agent taking a leading role in communication and operational management to support compliance and effective loan administration.</data>
