<edge source="&quot;LENDERS&quot;" target="&quot;BORROWER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">161.0</data>
  <data key="d5">The Borrower and the Lenders are engaged in a financial relationship governed by a credit agreement, which outlines their respective obligations and commitments. The Borrower is required to fulfill a variety of financial obligations to the Lenders, including repaying loans provided under the terms specified in the agreement. These obligations include repayment of the loan amounts, interest rates, and terms of repayment as delineated in the loan documents.

The Borrower is contractually obligated to repay the borrowed funds as stipulated, and must also make representations regarding its compliance and financial status to the Lenders. In addition to repayment, the Borrower is responsible for managing interactions with the Lenders through various borrowing mechanisms, including Revolving Loans, in accordance with the credit agreement. 

The Borrower has delivered financial statements to the Lenders prior to the Effective Date of the agreement, demonstrating compliance with the prerequisites for borrowing. Furthermore, the Borrower must ensure that if the Aggregate Outstanding Credit Exposure exceeds 105% of the Aggregate Commitment, they are obligated to repay extensions of credit to the Lenders.

The Lenders, on their part, are bound to the Borrower through commitments defined in the credit agreement, extending credit and providing financing while also monitoring the Borrower’s compliance with all outlined terms. The financial relationship involves ongoing dialogues; for instance, the Borrower has requested that the Lenders consider extending the Stated Maturity Date and Commitments. Overall, the relationship is characterized by a structured framework of financial obligations and mutual commitments, with both parties defined by the terms established in the agreement.</data>
