<edge source="&quot;BORROWER&quot;" target="&quot;ISSUING LENDER&quot;">
  <data key="d4">180.0</data>
  <data key="d5">The Issuing Lender extends credit to the Borrower, establishing a financial relationship characterized by mutual obligations. The Borrower borrows funds and may utilize letters of credit issued by the Issuing Lender to meet specific financial commitments. As part of the terms outlined in their credit agreement, the Borrower is required to fulfill repayment obligations, which include reimbursing the Issuing Lender for any amounts drawn under the letters of credit. This reimbursement must adhere to the terms specified in the agreement and includes any fees associated with the issuance of these letters of credit.

The Borrower is obliged to pay the Letter of Credit Fee and the Issuance Fee, which are directly related to the issuance of the letters of credit. Furthermore, the Borrower must agree on an amount for the issuance fee, and they are also required to pay amounts specified in issued certificates that determine reimbursement due to the Issuing Lender.

To obtain a letter of credit, the Borrower must submit an appropriate Letter of Credit Application to the Issuing Lender, who then guarantees payment to the letter’s beneficiary on behalf of the Borrower. Additionally, the Issuing Lender retains the right to request cash collateralization from the Borrower in case of a defaulting lender.

Through this agreement, the Issuing Lender not only issues letters of credit but also takes on the responsibility of making payments on behalf of the Borrower, thereby ensuring that the Borrower's financial obligations are met. The Issuing Lender is also tasked with informing the Borrower of any changes in law that may impact their financial obligations, thereby reinforcing the importance of their ongoing communication throughout the duration of the credit relationship.</data>
